1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:03,700
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] Hi, and welcome to Designing Open Democracy Podcast.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:11,720
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] This is actually an auto recording that we had on the 21st of October 2017 that we decided to show to you guys again.

3
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:19,880
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] And this event was called Citizens' Democracy, which was hosted by Brian Koo and Nicholas Morange.

4
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:28,420
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] As for their venue, the venue was gratefully sponsored to us by a co-working space called York Butter Factory at Melbourne.

5
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:41,220
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] And the speaker we will be showing you today is Nicholas Gruen, who's a prominent Australian economist, as well as a commentator on economic reforms, innovation and public policy.

6
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:59,800
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] He's currently the CEO of Lateral Economics and Economic Consultancy firms, covering economic reform and public policy, as well as a chairman of Open Knowledge Foundation Australia, who supports and empowers those who believe in open knowledge and transparency.

7
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:06,600
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] So a common thing you will find in his talk is how, essentially, current mass media is like mental junk food.

8
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:17,780
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] And what this leads to is essentially capturing the population's 30-second sound bite of what they observe, rather than the well-considered opinion of the population.

9
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:25,660
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] So a way of potentially solving this is through the use of citizen juries, which Hubert will explain in this coming talk.

10
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:34,800
[Narrator (Brian Khuu?)] So let's lead you in with an introduction that he will be speaking about himself and what he wants to explain about this idea of his.

11
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,480
[Nicholas Gruen] So in 2008, Barack Obama was elected.

12
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:49,420
[Nicholas Gruen] The most important thing that was happening at the time was that the world was sliding into the greatest economic catastrophe since the Great Depression.

13
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:53,460
[Nicholas Gruen] And the social media was a big deal.

14
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:57,000
[Nicholas Gruen] And so we held a policy brainstorming session.

15
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:00,660
[Nicholas Gruen] And you might not have heard of this very well.

16
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:02,840
[Nicholas Gruen] It didn't get a lot of publicity.

17
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:11,180
[Nicholas Gruen] But anyone could make a policy suggestion and they could vote other people's policy suggestion up and down the ladder.

18
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:21,180
[Nicholas Gruen] And so we were going to have true internet mediated direct democracy, at least by way of recommendation to the powers that be.

19
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:38,520
[Nicholas Gruen] What do you think was the suggestion that came first at a time like that for the US government, the suggestion that won the click baiting competition for voting policy up or down to legalise marijuana?

20
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:43,160
[Nicholas Gruen] The second one was to release Barack Obama's birth certificate.

21
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:45,720
[Nicholas Gruen] And spare a thought for the Americans.

22
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:54,900
[Nicholas Gruen] They have a fairly unique problem that it seems is much less of a problem in any other country, which of course is the abduction of their citizens by aliens.

23
00:02:56,040 --> 00:03:05,900
[Nicholas Gruen] So that's what came first, second and third in a very early attempt to bring the internet to bring us direct democracy.

24
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:08,960
[Nicholas Gruen] So welcome to what I call What's Pop Democracy.

25
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:11,240
[Nicholas Gruen] You'll be familiar with it.

26
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:12,820
[Nicholas Gruen] We live in it right now.

27
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:22,580
[Nicholas Gruen] And one way of summarizing will be to say that that is what the essential philosophy of our democracy is all about.

28
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:39,500
[Nicholas Gruen] Of course, you're not forced not to think before you vote, but the whole way in which media works is simply to try to arouse your attention, your emotions, your disgust, your resentment, and so on.

29
00:03:39,500 --> 00:04:01,520
[Nicholas Gruen] So we are now at a kind of extraordinary point where a policy which was to withdraw from, for the British to withdraw from Europe got up, although it's quite unclear what that meant or in what circumstances it would be regarded as a good idea and what circumstances would not be regarded as a good idea.

30
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:08,580
[Nicholas Gruen] Is the vote for Brexit, a vote for Brexit if the European Union won't do a deal?

31
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:16,940
[Nicholas Gruen] Is it a vote to simply jump out of the EU and take the tariff wall of the EU presents other countries?

32
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:21,040
[Nicholas Gruen] Or is it predicated on the idea that we ought to be able to do some kind of deal?

33
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:39,800
[Nicholas Gruen] Is it predicated on the idea that by coming out of Europe, Britain would say 350 million pounds a week, which could then be spent on the NHS, and buses driving around England claimed and so on, quite wrongly, and so on and so forth.

34
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:50,220
[Nicholas Gruen] And then we have our friend, Mr. Trump, a kind of, well, it's hard to describe Mr. Trump, but not really like anything we've ever seen before.

35
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:58,720
[Nicholas Gruen] Psychopathic, presumably, completely disorganised mental state, and there it is, the President of the United States.

36
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:16,300
[Nicholas Gruen] Now, in fact, I want to suggest to you that, just as Australia led the world on lots of things to do with policy, we were one of the world leaders in economic reform, and we did it better than most countries in the 80s, particularly in the 90s, we led the world on this as well.

37
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:49,600
[Nicholas Gruen] This is the man who led it, and though his reign is somewhat over now, we lived with the upshot of that, which is that the greatest achievement of the parliament that assembled in 2013 after the 2013 election was the elimination, or was the blasting away of a difficult policy consensus that had been reached over the previous 15 years to price carbon.

38
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:58,260
[Nicholas Gruen] How many people in this room would like to have a guess at what damage that does to the budget every year?

39
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:05,840
[Nicholas Gruen] Ten billion dollars.

40
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:12,340
[Nicholas Gruen] A third of the budget deficit every year because we abolished carbon pricing.

41
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:18,440
[Nicholas Gruen] What is significant about that policy is not that I'm against it and most of you will be against it, I think.

42
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:24,060
[Nicholas Gruen] What's significant about it is that most parliamentarians were against it.

43
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:28,880
[Nicholas Gruen] 80, 90 percent of parliamentarians thought it was a completely crazy thing to do.

44
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:41,140
[Nicholas Gruen] And somehow this system worked itself into a position of implementing it as the signal achievement of the incoming government, as is happening.

45
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:48,480
[Nicholas Gruen] And that's a way in which we beat the Brits to Brexit and the Americans to Donald Trump.

46
00:06:48,500 --> 00:07:00,160
[Nicholas Gruen] And one analogy I want to suggest to you is what's happening is that our society is being optimized for the things that evolved on the African Savannah.

47
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:06,260
[Nicholas Gruen] And I would say this is true of many industries, many aspects of our lives.

48
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:17,720
[Nicholas Gruen] And I want to suggest to you that one of the number of things that evolved on the African Savannah was that you didn't eat food that smelled in a way that disgusted you.

49
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:24,800
[Nicholas Gruen] You liked clean food, you liked food that was rich with fat, you liked sweet food.

50
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:26,840
[Nicholas Gruen] And that's what we've got.

51
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:38,760
[Nicholas Gruen] And food that is very close to being nutritious and has certainly been, a lot of loving care has been given to what it is, is now poisonous.

52
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:42,100
[Nicholas Gruen] And that's roughly what's happening in democracy.

53
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:47,760
[Nicholas Gruen] We are getting pretty much exactly what we're asking for in certain kinds of respects.

54
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:58,720
[Nicholas Gruen] That's an uncomfortable thing to say, but otherwise, unless you want to play the game, which most people do, that's all those nasty politicians of We're the Good Guys.

55
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:03,000
[Nicholas Gruen] I don't think that will get you anywhere, but that's how the game is played.

56
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,620
[Nicholas Gruen] We're part of this just as we buy McDonald's handbills.

57
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:11,040
[Nicholas Gruen] I want to suggest two very simple ideas to you about democracy.

58
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:17,760
[Nicholas Gruen] These both come from a rather patrician fellow, quite right-wing.

59
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:24,000
[Nicholas Gruen] They don't have their sort of logical ideas, so you can dispense with whether they're right-wing or left-wing.

60
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,340
[Nicholas Gruen] They're basically logical ideas.

61
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:29,580
[Nicholas Gruen] Joseph Schumpeter is the man.

62
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:41,039
[Nicholas Gruen] He was the Finance Minister of Austria, as our other speaker might know, was a professor at Harvard for quite a long time and a great historian of economics and a great economist himself.

63
00:08:41,039 --> 00:08:50,640
[Nicholas Gruen] Here are the two ideas that stood out for me when I read the book about 40 years ago and then I reread the two chapters on democracy.

64
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:52,860
[Nicholas Gruen] Very simple architectural ideas.

65
00:08:52,960 --> 00:09:02,720
[Nicholas Gruen] There's the first one that one of the fundamental issues of democracy is to navigate the Cognitive Division of Labor.

66
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:09,020
[Nicholas Gruen] And in many senses, the Cognitive Division of Labor is kind of the serpent in paradise.

67
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:14,840
[Nicholas Gruen] Because, of course, a Cognitive Division of Labor introduces principal age and problems.

68
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:22,480
[Nicholas Gruen] It introduces the need to trust certain people to act on your behalf, meanwhile they have your own interests.

69
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:26,500
[Nicholas Gruen] So, that's the first idea.

70
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:31,620
[Nicholas Gruen] Schumpeter uses the expression leadership.

71
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:33,240
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm not going to worry about that too much.

72
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:40,940
[Nicholas Gruen] The basic idea I'm taking from it is the idea of a cognitive division like that.

73
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:45,900
[Nicholas Gruen] That even quite simple social organizations have to break themselves up.

74
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:51,480
[Nicholas Gruen] So, if you look at any club, it's got a treasurer, a secretary, a president, and so on.

75
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,300
[Nicholas Gruen] That's basically the idea.

76
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:56,700
[Nicholas Gruen] And the second idea is this.

77
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:07,680
[Nicholas Gruen] That if you go down to the shop to buy bread, you will engage your rational faculties.

78
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:09,700
[Nicholas Gruen] You will know something about what you want.

79
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:12,220
[Nicholas Gruen] You'll have a lot of background experience in it.

80
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:25,020
[Nicholas Gruen] You will be trying to attend to a practical question, which is, what's the best bread I can get, as far as sort of divided by value of month for month, if you know what I mean.

81
00:10:25,020 --> 00:10:27,680
[Nicholas Gruen] That's not the world that we're in in politics.

82
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:42,380
[Nicholas Gruen] In politics, we actually don't, there's no rational reason for us to vote at all, in terms of our self-interest, because we have an infinitesimal chance of influencing the result.

83
00:10:42,380 --> 00:10:53,440
[Nicholas Gruen] So we're actually about something else in politics, and politics, I will assert to you, based on this authority and your own experience, is essentially run by emotion.

84
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:57,480
[Nicholas Gruen] That's the motive force behind it.

85
00:10:57,720 --> 00:10:58,740
[Audience] The motive force.

86
00:10:58,740 --> 00:10:59,180
[Nicholas Gruen] Sorry?

87
00:10:59,180 --> 00:11:00,180
[Audience] The motive force.

88
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:00,920
[Nicholas Gruen] Well, thank you.

89
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:03,080
[Nicholas Gruen] Yes, I should investigate the...

90
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:06,200
[Nicholas Gruen] I should investigate the etymology of that.

91
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:09,700
[Nicholas Gruen] So, those emotions can operate for good.

92
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:12,920
[Nicholas Gruen] And I'm going to call these feminine emotions.

93
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:20,800
[Nicholas Gruen] And this is the emotion of binding us together from within, as it were.

94
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:26,780
[Nicholas Gruen] And there is also something that goes pretty horribly wrong.

95
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:33,300
[Nicholas Gruen] And that's typically emotions which take a strongly masculine character.

96
00:11:33,300 --> 00:11:36,440
[Nicholas Gruen] Now, I'm not saying masculine bad, feminine good.

97
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:42,940
[Nicholas Gruen] But I am saying masculine is much more likely to come with some pretty nasty costs.

98
00:11:42,940 --> 00:11:44,900
[Nicholas Gruen] Feminine can go wrong too.

99
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:54,560
[Nicholas Gruen] But at the moment, I want to suggest to you that we have a polity which is massively pushed in the direction of one set of emotions.

100
00:11:55,900 --> 00:12:00,620
[Nicholas Gruen] So, here is something that somebody once said.

101
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:06,780
[Nicholas Gruen] They were a, they are, I suppose, a captain in the Australian Navy.

102
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:10,740
[Nicholas Gruen] It was quite a joy to hold those little kids' hands and watch them smile.

103
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:14,760
[Nicholas Gruen] You know, who said, well, what's the event that person's talking about?

104
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:21,720
[Nicholas Gruen] It's the same event as that expression.

105
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:33,760
[Nicholas Gruen] And the first expression was said first, and it was made clear to Commander Norman Banks that it wasn't appreciated by his political masters.

106
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:45,080
[Nicholas Gruen] And what happened in our politics is that one particular type of emotion came to dominate.

107
00:12:46,840 --> 00:12:56,000
[Nicholas Gruen] And the problem is most of the things we're stuck worrying about are things that require quite a lot of thought.

108
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,340
[Nicholas Gruen] They'll require compromise and so on.

109
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:05,340
[Nicholas Gruen] And we have a style of political discussion which actually makes that very difficult.

110
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:12,100
[Nicholas Gruen] It doesn't help that the size of soundbites are kind of collapsing steadily.

111
00:13:12,100 --> 00:13:17,240
[Nicholas Gruen] And we have a style of politics, which I call politics as culture war.

112
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:22,020
[Nicholas Gruen] You know you're on a particular side, you know you're the good guys, and then there are the bad guys.

113
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:29,620
[Nicholas Gruen] And we play, you know, we police their speech, we do the whole, it's the works.

114
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:42,780
[Nicholas Gruen] So, and then this is then, this is then strengthened by our press, which plays on emotion and all of the toxic kind of emotions.

115
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:43,960
[Nicholas Gruen] This is in Britain.

116
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:46,520
[Nicholas Gruen] I was in London and I saw that poster.

117
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:47,960
[Nicholas Gruen] I was just horrified.

118
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:53,880
[Nicholas Gruen] I mean, here are wealthy people tearing up the social contract that allows them to be wealthy.

119
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:59,820
[Nicholas Gruen] The rule of law, that they are, they're enemies of the people, a fine, Stalinist expression.

120
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:06,120
[Nicholas Gruen] And the same sort of thing has been happening in this country for years and so on.

121
00:14:06,120 --> 00:14:08,460
[Nicholas Gruen] This is a nice little vignette for you.

122
00:14:08,460 --> 00:14:16,080
[Nicholas Gruen] Remember when the Indonesians blew away to Australians for smuggling drugs?

123
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:24,520
[Nicholas Gruen] And of course, the Daily Telegraph were horrified and orchestrated national outrage that the Indonesians should be served callous.

124
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:30,340
[Nicholas Gruen] But of course, when they've been caught, the Daily Telegraph was on the other side.

125
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:33,760
[Nicholas Gruen] You know, throw the book at them, that's what they deserve and so on.

126
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:37,680
[Nicholas Gruen] So not a word of that, of course.

127
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:39,980
[Nicholas Gruen] Power without responsibility.

128
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:48,740
[Nicholas Gruen] This is when you have the temerity to say that there should be some regulatory system which holds the media to some kind of account.

129
00:14:48,740 --> 00:14:56,340
[Nicholas Gruen] And so here we are with politics very much in this state and it's now official.

130
00:14:56,340 --> 00:15:00,000
[Nicholas Gruen] So what might we do about it?

131
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,640
[Nicholas Gruen] That's one of the things we need to do about it.

132
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:08,620
[Nicholas Gruen] We need, I think, to rebalance the way what kinds of emotions dominate in political debate.

133
00:15:08,620 --> 00:15:13,380
[Nicholas Gruen] And I wish to turn to the origins of democracy to talk about that.

134
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:15,960
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm not going to talk very much about participation.

135
00:15:15,960 --> 00:15:17,900
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm going to talk about deliberation.

136
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:23,540
[Nicholas Gruen] And there's a whole sort of divide we could talk about there in questions or at some other time.

137
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:27,000
[Nicholas Gruen] So here are these two ways of representing.

138
00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:42,800
[Nicholas Gruen] So if we take Schumpeter's idea that we need a division of labor, but we can't have everyone having a view about everything any more than we would want everyone to have a view about every criminal case that goes before the courts.

139
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:48,880
[Nicholas Gruen] Then we need to represent the people and we need to have what I call cognitive elites.

140
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:56,260
[Nicholas Gruen] They don't have to be more powerful or more important than anyone else, but they need to spend the time trying to think about the issues.

141
00:15:56,260 --> 00:16:05,540
[Nicholas Gruen] We can do this by election or we can do it by law, which is the way we do it in juries, which is something like random selection or some similar process.

142
00:16:05,940 --> 00:16:16,040
[Nicholas Gruen] It comes as a big surprise to a lot of people to know that the idea of having elections being a touchstone of democracy is a fairly recent idea.

143
00:16:16,040 --> 00:16:22,760
[Nicholas Gruen] It goes back to the French Revolution and the American Revolution in the late 18th century.

144
00:16:22,760 --> 00:16:28,160
[Nicholas Gruen] Before that, democracy was much more associated with selection by lot.

145
00:16:28,160 --> 00:16:37,840
[Nicholas Gruen] Going back to Athens, the Athenian boule, European cities like Venice and Lawrence during the Renaissance, juries in court and so on.

146
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:49,300
[Nicholas Gruen] And here are some things about the different kind of politics that takes place.

147
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:56,780
[Nicholas Gruen] Elections, this is something that the more I've thought about, the more important I think it is, and I never really see people talk about it.

148
00:16:56,800 --> 00:17:00,220
[Nicholas Gruen] This is again a very simple logical point.

149
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:05,819
[Nicholas Gruen] If you want to represent people by elections, that is a competitive process.

150
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:14,300
[Nicholas Gruen] You cannot beat a politician without beating other politicians to the task of representing the people.

151
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:22,300
[Nicholas Gruen] And once you become a representative, you then, in your party, try and beat your opponents.

152
00:17:22,300 --> 00:17:29,040
[Nicholas Gruen] It's competitive and it is aristocratic, and according to both Aristotle and Montesquieu.

153
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:34,940
[Nicholas Gruen] Now, it has a democratic sanction because the people voted for it, but think about the people in parliament.

154
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:53,000
[Nicholas Gruen] How many of them, how they represent the age profile of the population, they are skewed in a particular way, and how they represent the education profile and the wealth profile of the population, and you can see the idea of it being aristocratic.

155
00:17:53,000 --> 00:18:00,500
[Nicholas Gruen] Here we have a unitary system where people's main problem is to try and get to an agreement.

156
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:15,060
[Nicholas Gruen] In a jury, your job isn't to beat anyone, it's to try and come up with a conclusion that works, and your natural instinct is to compromise it so far as you can manage it to get to some sort of agreement that everyone is happy with.

157
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,300
[Nicholas Gruen] And it is democratic in this sense.

158
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:28,860
[Nicholas Gruen] And here is a Greek word for you, which I think is really a fundamental word, and it doesn't mean freedom of speech, though it's sometimes translated as such, it means equality of speech.

159
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:50,040
[Nicholas Gruen] And one of the reasons I would argue that people like Pauline Hanson are doing as well as they are, is that Pauline Hanson, whether you like her or not, is an authentic politician, and people hear their voice much more through Pauline Hanson than they do through the brand management of many of the other parties.

160
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:55,600
[Nicholas Gruen] So the idea is then to say, we should be using this kind of mechanism much more.

161
00:18:55,600 --> 00:19:00,760
[Nicholas Gruen] Now there's been lots of experimentation with these mechanisms.

162
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:05,740
[Nicholas Gruen] There's a lot of field work which tells you that they work pretty well, and I won't.

163
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:10,980
[Nicholas Gruen] I will now race through some of these things, but I'll just show you this one.

164
00:19:10,980 --> 00:19:13,160
[Nicholas Gruen] I think this is a really important point.

165
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:22,800
[Nicholas Gruen] This was a Citizens' Jury about making Adelaide safe and prosperous, have a good nightlife that was safe.

166
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:26,400
[Nicholas Gruen] And imagine that being discussed on the radio.

167
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:33,600
[Nicholas Gruen] The six foot two man and the two short women, well, they'd come in on the radio and they'd have a good old barn.

168
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:34,960
[Nicholas Gruen] That's how we do it.

169
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:39,360
[Nicholas Gruen] And this guy walks in and he goes, oh, I see, I see, there's a problem.

170
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:40,580
[Nicholas Gruen] How would I know?

171
00:19:40,600 --> 00:19:43,340
[Nicholas Gruen] And they rapidly come to a conclusion.

172
00:19:43,340 --> 00:19:55,900
[Nicholas Gruen] The upshot of this is that juries, the people on Citizens Juries almost invariably report the experience is a fantastically good one.

173
00:19:55,900 --> 00:20:06,020
[Nicholas Gruen] The Citizens' Jury in Melbourne of the 43 or however many people were on the Citizens' Jury, two or possibly three people stood for local council at the next election.

174
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:12,680
[Nicholas Gruen] That gives you some idea of how that, of how generative it is to this kind of activity.

175
00:20:12,700 --> 00:20:22,240
[Nicholas Gruen] And so what we've got here is the kind of context in which this kind of sentiment might have a better chance.

176
00:20:22,880 --> 00:20:35,480
[Nicholas Gruen] So I'll skip through that, because I'm getting a wind up, but I will tell you what I want to get at, a sort of utopia for me, or where I would like to, you know, you make me the nebulous dictator, this is what you'll get.

177
00:20:35,480 --> 00:20:37,940
[Nicholas Gruen] And then I'll talk about how I think we should get there.

178
00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:46,380
[Nicholas Gruen] I think we should acknowledge that we are in a fantastic world that has been built over the last 200 years since the French and American revolutions.

179
00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:49,840
[Nicholas Gruen] It's a massive success with some very mousy toxic bits in it.

180
00:20:50,500 --> 00:21:15,020
[Nicholas Gruen] And so, where the 18th century and the 19th century was about setting up checks and balances between a lower house representing the people and an upper house representing property, I think we should be now moving, and we will now move towards checks and balances between the people as represented by their elected representatives and the people represented as they are in a jury.

181
00:21:15,020 --> 00:21:16,700
[Nicholas Gruen] Now, how can we get there?

182
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:29,000
[Nicholas Gruen] Well, I think it's not that hard to get there, because this would be a fantastically powerful form of political activism.

183
00:21:29,000 --> 00:22:10,020
[Nicholas Gruen] If you imagine Tony Abbott promising to abolish carbon pricing, and a Citizens' Jury is funded, and a Citizens' Chamber exists, the media would report it, it would be political news, and I have great confidence that when people concentrated on the two policies and found that one was a policy, the one that was for carbon pricing, and the other one was really a kind of set of sound bites to get through a radio interview, and that it was going to cost them, they were going to have to come up with another $10 billion a year of tax money to get rid of this thing, they would have voted for, they would have prevented.

184
00:22:10,920 --> 00:22:18,460
[Nicholas Gruen] So I think the split would have been something like 60, 70% pro-carbon pricing at a time like that.

185
00:22:18,460 --> 00:22:30,740
[Nicholas Gruen] That, I think, would be on its own very politically powerful, and a fine piece of legitimation for the sort of world that I want to move towards.

186
00:22:30,740 --> 00:22:42,900
[Nicholas Gruen] That's a list of things that I'd be prepared to defend at some stage, the qualities of the democracy we have now, and I'll just finish with a couple of points on this.

187
00:22:45,860 --> 00:22:47,320
[Nicholas Gruen] I've actually intimated this before.

188
00:22:47,320 --> 00:22:52,320
[Nicholas Gruen] A lot of discussion about democracy involves a magic trick.

189
00:22:52,320 --> 00:22:55,660
[Nicholas Gruen] The magic trick is that the politicians are letting us down.

190
00:22:55,660 --> 00:22:58,220
[Nicholas Gruen] The politicians are the bad guys, we're always the good guys.

191
00:22:58,820 --> 00:23:06,060
[Nicholas Gruen] So, we the people always, it disappears like the lady here, whenever we the people need to disappear.

192
00:23:06,060 --> 00:23:15,400
[Nicholas Gruen] When some dirty work is done, like keeping people out of our country, that's the nasty politicians that do that, not the people who vote for the nasty politicians.

193
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:22,240
[Nicholas Gruen] And I think that really damages a lot of thinking about how to fix democracy.

194
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:23,760
[Nicholas Gruen] And I'll finish on this point.

195
00:23:24,220 --> 00:23:28,700
[Nicholas Gruen] Here's a friend of mine, Joe Trippi, talking about this is in 2007.

196
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:51,940
[Nicholas Gruen] He engineered the Howard Dean campaign, and he was tremendously optimistic about the power of the internet because it was going to bring the people into politics, which, you know, if you remember what Bernard says in Yes Minister, Prime Minister Hacker says to him, it seems that civil service exists just to prevent politicians implementing the sacred promises they've made to the people.

197
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:55,000
[Nicholas Gruen] And Bernard says, well, somebody has to.

198
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:59,140
[Nicholas Gruen] So anyway, Joe and I had part of a bet at this point when I said he was wrong.

199
00:23:59,140 --> 00:24:01,280
[Nicholas Gruen] I think he thinks he was wrong.

200
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:04,200
[Nicholas Gruen] But a great deal, so this is much more risk.

201
00:24:04,200 --> 00:24:05,420
[Nicholas Gruen] I should have a date on that.

202
00:24:05,420 --> 00:24:14,900
[Nicholas Gruen] But a great deal of the activism to fix democracy is based on this idea that we, the people, need to take charge.

203
00:24:14,900 --> 00:24:16,440
[Nicholas Gruen] That was the slogan for Brexit.

204
00:24:16,440 --> 00:24:17,760
[Nicholas Gruen] That was the slogan for Trump.

205
00:24:17,900 --> 00:24:19,920
[Nicholas Gruen] That was the slogan for carbon price.

206
00:24:20,240 --> 00:24:22,720
[Nicholas Gruen] It wasn't the slogan for carbon price, and I was lying to you.

207
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:30,280
[Nicholas Gruen] But it just shows you, or pay attention when people are getting into demagogue mode.

208
00:24:30,280 --> 00:24:36,160
[Nicholas Gruen] So here, the founder of Medium and Twitter says, he's going to fix it all up.

209
00:24:36,160 --> 00:24:38,960
[Nicholas Gruen] He's realized that things don't work.

210
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:40,600
[Nicholas Gruen] So I tweeted this.

211
00:24:41,040 --> 00:24:52,400
[Nicholas Gruen] But here is another, I'll just show you one other invention in the hyperventilating space of Silicon Valley entrepreneurs who are going to save us from democracy.

212
00:24:52,420 --> 00:24:54,700
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm leading Lyft to fix politics.

213
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:55,340
[Nicholas Gruen] Here's how.

214
00:24:55,340 --> 00:24:57,160
[Nicholas Gruen] So he wrote all this stuff up.

215
00:24:57,160 --> 00:25:07,320
[Nicholas Gruen] And always the analogy is that it's all about the app, and the app's going to, and to put it in economic joke, and the app's going to lower transactions costs and won't that be terrific?

216
00:25:07,320 --> 00:25:09,000
[Nicholas Gruen] And that will bring the people in.

217
00:25:09,040 --> 00:25:21,900
[Nicholas Gruen] And of course, the story I've told you is that actually, we need a few more transactions costs in there, that the transactions costs that we've got in there about electing representatives are somehow going wrong.

218
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:23,960
[Nicholas Gruen] And I'm proposing a way to fix that.

219
00:25:23,960 --> 00:25:26,140
[Nicholas Gruen] Anyway, so here are the comments.

220
00:25:26,140 --> 00:25:28,320
[Nicholas Gruen] Proud to know you and admire what you're doing.

221
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:29,680
[Nicholas Gruen] Let me know how we can help.

222
00:25:29,680 --> 00:25:30,360
[Nicholas Gruen] Thanks, Mary.

223
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:31,640
[Nicholas Gruen] Appreciate the support.

224
00:25:31,640 --> 00:25:33,560
[Nicholas Gruen] We need more people like you in the world.

225
00:25:33,560 --> 00:25:36,520
[Nicholas Gruen] Excited to see what excellent progress things are.

226
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:42,880
[Nicholas Gruen] And every one of these, you go girl, comments gets a reply.

227
00:25:42,880 --> 00:25:44,360
[Nicholas Gruen] I popped in.

228
00:25:44,360 --> 00:25:48,480
[Nicholas Gruen] I wish you all the best with your adventure, though I fear you're playing by the rules of the existing system.

229
00:25:48,480 --> 00:25:50,160
[Nicholas Gruen] And I made an argument.

230
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:56,820
[Nicholas Gruen] Life went on.

231
00:25:56,820 --> 00:25:58,040
[Nicholas Gruen] He wasn't interested in that.

232
00:25:59,020 --> 00:26:00,740
[Nicholas Gruen] He's in automatic pilot mode.

233
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:01,480
[Nicholas Gruen] Maybe it will work.

234
00:26:01,480 --> 00:26:02,400
[Nicholas Gruen] Who knows?

235
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:05,160
[Nicholas Gruen] So I will leave you there.

236
00:26:05,160 --> 00:26:13,260
[Nicholas Gruen] But I think if you, if anyone tells you this is a solution, get them also to define the problem and give it a good shake and see.

237
00:26:13,260 --> 00:26:17,660
[Nicholas Gruen] I mean, they have to, the solution might not be a solution.

238
00:26:17,660 --> 00:26:27,820
[Nicholas Gruen] But often, so the solutions people want to build, the solutions that got, got introduced for other problems like finding a date and various other things.

239
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:28,560
[Nicholas Gruen] So I'll leave it there.

240
00:26:28,780 --> 00:26:31,200
[Nicholas Gruen] Okay, and thank you very much.

